  "Question": "A boat travels \\(12\\) kilometers perpendicularly away from the shore, then travels \\(5\\) kilometers in a perpendicular direction. What is its straight-line distance from the starting point, in kilometers?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "13",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Under the recorded interpretation that the second travel leg is perpendicular to the first, the straight-line distance is the hypotenuse of a right triangle. By the Pythagorean theorem, it is\n\\[\n\\sqrt{12^2+5^2}=\\sqrt{169}=13\\text{ km}.\n\\]\nThe wording does not explicitly say what the second direction is perpendicular to. If it is again perpendicular to the shore, continuing away gives \\(12+5=17\\) km, while returning toward the shore gives \\(12-5=7\\) km. Thus the recorded answer is \\(13\\) km under its stated interpretation, but the wording alone does not determine a unique distance.",
